I'll explore the express repo structure to find how routing is wired up.This confirms `router` is an external npm dependency (`"router": "^2.2.0"`), not implemented in this repo. Let me verify how `application.js` wires it up.Confirmed: `lib/express.js` does `var Router = require('router')` — the router is imported from the external `router` npm package (not implemented in this repo), and `exports.Router = Router` / `exports.Route = Router.Route` re-export it directly.

Now let's look at `lib/application.js` for how the app wires the router into request handling (`app.handle`, `lazyrouter`, `app.use`, `app.METHOD` etc.).This is enough evidence. Let me also check the HTTP method registration (`app.get`, `app.post`, etc.) further down the file to complete the picture.I have everything needed to answer.

## Answer

**Application-level route handling is wired up in `lib/application.js`**, in the `express` application prototype:

- **`app.init()`** (lib/application.js, near line ~59) lazily creates the router instance via a getter on `this.router`:
  ```js
  Object.defineProperty(this, 'router', {
    get: function getrouter() {
      if (router === null) {
        router = new Router({ caseSensitive: ..., strict: ... });
      }
      return router;
    }
  });
  ```
- **`app.handle(req, res, callback)`** is the entry point invoked for every incoming request (the app itself is `function(req, res, next) { app.handle(req, res, next); }`, set up in `lib/express.js`'s `createApplication()`). It sets up `req`/`res` prototypes, then delegates dispatch to the router: `this.router.handle(req, res, done);`
- **`app.use(fn)`** proxies to `router.use(path, fn)`, registering middleware/sub-apps on the same router instance.
- **`app.route(path)`** proxies to `router.route(path)`.
- HTTP verb shortcuts (**`app.get`, `app.post`, `app.put`, ... and `app.all`**), generated via a `methods.forEach(...)` loop near the bottom of `lib/application.js`, all internally call `this.route(path)` and then invoke the corresponding method on the returned `Route` object.

**The router itself is NOT implemented in this repository.** It is imported as an external npm dependency:

- `lib/express.js`: `var Router = require('router');` and later `exports.Route = Router.Route; exports.Router = Router;` — Express literally re-exports the third-party package's constructors as its own `express.Router`/`express.Route`.
- `lib/application.js`: `var Router = require('router');` — same import, used to construct `this.router`.
- `package.json` dependencies list: `"router": "^2.2.0"` — confirming it's a published npm package (https://github.com/pillarjs/router, part of the pillarjs/expressjs ecosystem), not source code inside `expressjs/express`.

There is no `lib/router` directory or router source file in this repo (confirmed via the top-level `lib/` tree: `application.js`, `express.js`, `request.js`, `response.js`, `utils.js`, `view.js` — no router implementation file).

**What I did not verify:** I did not fetch/read the actual source of the `router` npm package (it lives in a separate repository, likely `pillarjs/router` or `expressjs/router`) — only confirmed that Express treats it as an external dependency rather than embedding its implementation. I also did not exhaustively read `lib/request.js`/`lib/response.js` since they weren't relevant to the wiring question.

